        "url":  "https://www.technologyreview.com/2026/07/20/1140655/ai-biases-hiring-humans/",
        "date":  "2026-07-20",
        "theme":  "Philosophy",
        "summary":  "Researchers from Princeton and University of Chicago found that large language models develop stronger hiring biases than humans when making repeated hiring decisions. In a simulated hiring game, AI models quickly stereotyped job candidates by ethnic group, with newer reasoning models showing particularly pronounced bias. The models segregated candidates into different job categories based on limited early data, demonstrating that AI can create novel biases beyond those learned from training data.",
        "execSummary":  "Large language models used for hiring screening exhibit significantly higher stereotyping tendencies than human evaluators, with newer reasoning models like OpenAI o3 showing bias levels 65% above human participants. The research reveals that AI systems generate novel biases through repeated decision-making rather than solely inheriting them from training data, creating compounding discrimination risk in any enterprise workflow that uses LLMs iteratively. Organizations deploying AI in HR, legal, or high-stakes operational contexts should immediately audit for emergent bias patterns and implement incentive structures that reward diverse outcomes. Failing to act creates material legal and reputational exposure as regulatory scrutiny of algorithmic discrimination intensifies.",
