        "title":  "AI agents are not your \u0027coworkers\u0027",
        "source":  "MIT Technology Review",
        "url":  "https://www.technologyreview.com/2026/06/29/1139849/ai-agents-are-not-your-coworkers/",
        "date":  "2026-06-29",
        "theme":  "Agents",
        "summary":  "Research by Boston University professor Emma Wiles demonstrates that framing AI agents as employees rather than software tools reduces error detection by 18% and increases inappropriate delegation of responsibility. Tech companies like Microsoft, OpenAI, and Google are marketing AI agents as digital colleagues, but this branding creates unrealistic expectations and undermines human accountability in critical sectors including healthcare, warfare, and government.",
        "execSummary":  "The coworker framing pushed by major AI vendors is measurably harmful to enterprise performance: workers who perceive AI as an employee catch significantly fewer errors and over-escalate decisions rather than exercising judgment. This is not a soft cultural concern ΓÇö it is a documented accuracy and accountability risk that affects any enterprise deploying agentic AI in operational workflows. Leaders should establish internal communication and training standards that frame AI as a powerful tool requiring human oversight, not a peer with autonomous judgment. Organizations that maintain clear human accountability structures will be better positioned for regulatory compliance and will outperform those that allow the coworker narrative to erode responsibility boundaries.",
